e boundary.\n\n## The ruling you asked for on `bleedout.outcome`\n\nThe orchestrator raised it off the s21 smoke: `outcome` is `\"death\"` on a pawn whose `ticks` is `null` (reads 05 and 11).\n\n**CORRECT AS DESIGNED. The suite asserts it.**\n\n`outcome` answers *what happens WHEN the clock runs out* — a property of the pawn's DEATH PATH, computed from `Pawn_HealthTracker.ShouldBeDead` and the Deathless branch above it, and true of a pawn who is not bleeding at all: a Deathless pawn with an intact brain reports `coma` whether or not anything is currently killing it. `ticks` is the only field that says whether there IS a deadline, and it says so unambiguously by being `null`.\n\nNulling `outcome` alongside `ticks` would make `null` mean both \"no deadline\" and \"could not compute\" — precisely the ambiguity the block exists to remove, and the same argument that made `ticks` `null` rather than `int.MaxValue`.\n\nSo **1.1h** requires `outcome ∈ {none, coma, death}` on a pawn with no clock at all, and **1.1i** pins it to `death` for a plain colonist. Recorded in the `.md`.\n\n## What the suite does that is not a check\n\nThree FINDINGS print as their own line and are repeated in the summary, deliberately NOT as checks — the exit code answers \"were the acceptance bullets met\", and a suite that goes permanently red over a metadata string teaches the next session to ignore its own colour. Two belong to `40ed42f` and are on that issue; none belongs here. **Nothing in `PawnSerializer.cs`'s half of this round read wrong to a sceptical eye.**\n\n## Fixture traps the suite encodes rather than discovers\n\n* `pawn` takes `id`, not `pawn`.\n* **`dev:heal`'s default `mode:\"injuries\"` does NOT clear `BloodLoss`** and leaves it RISING — but `mode:\"full\"` does, since it drops every `isBad` hediff. The suite never heals; it uses a fresh subject per role.\n* `dev:damage {mode:\"amount\"}` stops at Downed by construction, so small `amount` and repeated calls is the only route to 